{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kung-Hsiang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kung-Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yutong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiming","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Caiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Sheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chien-Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GUI-KV: Efficient GUI Agents via KV Cache with Spatio-Temporal Awareness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T05:37:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:30:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-10-07T23:23:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-09T00:19:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Graphical user interface (GUI) agents built on vision-language models have emerged as a promising approach to automate human-computer workflows. However, they also face the inefficiency challenge as they process long sequences of high-resolution screenshots and solving long-horizon tasks, making inference slow, costly and memory-bound. While key-value (KV) caching can mitigate this, storing the full cache is prohibitive for image-heavy contexts. Existing cache-compression methods are sub-optimal as they do not account for the spatial and temporal redundancy of GUIs. In this work, we first analyze attention patterns in GUI agent workloads and find that, unlike in natural images, attention sparsity is uniformly high across all transformer layers. This insight motivates a simple uniform budget allocation strategy, which we show empirically outperforms more complex layer-varying schemes. Building on this, we introduce GUI-KV, a plug-and-play KV cache compression method for GUI agents that requires no retraining. GUI-KV combines two novel techniques: (i) spatial saliency guidance, which augments attention scores with the L2 norm of hidden states to better preserve semantically important visual tokens, and (ii) temporal redundancy scoring, which projects previous frames' keys onto the current frame's key subspace to preferentially prune redundant history. Across standard GUI agent benchmarks and models, GUI-KV outperforms competitive KV compression baselines, closely matching full-cache accuracy at modest budgets. Notably, in a 5-screenshot setting on the AgentNetBench benchmark, GUI-KV reduces decoding FLOPs by 38.9% while increasing step accuracy by 4.1% over the full-cache baseline. These results demonstrate that exploiting GUI-specific redundancies enables efficient and reliable agent performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by TMLR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:02:15Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:02:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-09T03:15:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruqian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijiao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yijiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongyi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Annie","familyName":"Qu","name":"Qu, Annie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Syntax-Guided Diffusion Language Models with User-Integrated Personalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:33:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:02:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-22T13:51:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-25T00:31:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models have made revolutionary progress in generating human-like text, yet their outputs often tend to be generic, exhibiting insufficient structural diversity, which limits personalized expression. Recent advances in diffusion models have opened new opportunities for improving language generation beyond the limitations of autoregressive paradigms. In this work, we propose a syntax-guided diffusion language model that integrates structural supervision and personalized conditioning to enhance text quality, diversity, and controllability. We introduce a cascaded framework that generates syntactic guidance before conditional text generation, and further generalize it to a novel noncascaded architecture for better alignment between structure and content. By incorporating syntactic information in the generating process, the proposed model better captures the lexical and structural characteristics of stylistic sentence construction. To enable fine-grained personalization, we develop a shared representation mechanism that facilitates information integration across users, supporting both faithful stylistic generation and generalizable zero-shot inference. Extensive experiments on multiple tasks demonstrate the superiority of our approach in fluency, diversity, and stylistic fidelity. Further qualitative analyses highlight its interpretability and flexibility in learning personalized patterns."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:14:48Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:14:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-25T03:50:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00037","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00037","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00037","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Jianing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenhong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhenhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiyao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yiyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangqi","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Xiangqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiqian","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhiqian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuning","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanpei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuanpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qi","familyName":"Dou","name":"Dou, Qi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaodong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yaodong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianglong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xianglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huijie","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Huijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weifeng","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Weifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Simin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RobustVLA: On Robustness of Vision-Language-Action Model against Multi-Modal Perturbations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T14:42:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T08:40:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:36:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T09:55:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:52:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T07:21:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-25T01:28:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-07-19T04:43:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:48:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-07-27T06:20:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-07-28T01:15:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In Vision-Language-Actionf(VLA) models, robustness to real-world perturbations is critical for deployment. Existing methods target simple visual disturbances, overlooking the broader multi-modal perturbations that arise in actions, instructions, environments, and observations. Here, we first evaluate the robustness of mainstream VLAs under 17 perturbations across four modalities. We find (1) actions as the most fragile modality, (2) Existing visual-robust VLA do not gain robustness in other modality, and (3) pi0 demonstrates superior robustness. To build multi-modal robust VLAs, we propose RobustVLA against perturbations in VLA inputs and outputs. For output robustness, we perform offline robust optimization against worst-case action noise that maximizes mismatch in flow matching objective. This can be seen as adversarial training, label smoothing, and outlier penalization. For input robustness, we enforce consistent actions across input variations that preserve task semantics. To account for multiple perturbations, we formulate robustness as a multi-armed bandit problem and apply an upper confidence bound algorithm to automatically identify the most harmful noise. Experiments on LIBERO demonstrate our RobustVLA delivers absolute gains over baselines of 12.6% on the pi0 backbone and 10.4% on the OpenVLA backbone across all 17 perturbations, achieving 50.6x faster inference than existing visual-robust BYOVLA that requires external LLMs, and a 10.4% gain under mixed perturbations. On the real-world FR5 robot, under four types of multimodal perturbations, RobustVLA shows strong low-data performance, outperforming pi0 by 65.6% success rate with 25 demonstrations. Even with abundant demos, our method still outperform pi0 by 30% success rate. Code and demo videos available at https://github.com/gakakulicc/RobustVLA."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00037","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":6,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:53Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-28T03:48:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01167","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01167","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01167","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiran","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Yiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prithviraj","familyName":"Ammanabrolu","name":"Ammanabrolu, Prithviraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Simultaneous Multi-objective Alignment Across Verifiable and Non-verifiable Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:54:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-30T06:40:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T00:30:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Aligning large language models to human preferences is inherently multidimensional, yet most pipelines collapse heterogeneous signals into a single objective. We seek to answer what it would take to simultaneously align a model across various domains spanning those with: verifiable rewards, non-verifiable subjective preferences, and complex interactive scenarios. Such multi-objective alignment setups are often plagued by individual objectives being at odds with each other, resulting in inefficient training and limited user control during inference. To address these issues, we propose $\\textbf{M}$ulti-$\\textbf{A}$ction-$\\textbf{H}$ead $\\textbf{AL}$ignment with PRM-guided Dec$\\textbf{O}$ding ($\\textbf{MAHALO}$), a unified framework that standardizes PRM training across verifiable and non-verifiable settings for step-level supervision, performs vectorized multi-objective alignment with Multi-Action-Head DPO, and enables controllable inference through objective-specific weighting and PRM-guided decoding. Experiments across math reasoning, human values alignment, and multi-turn tutoring show that MAHALO jointly improves multiple objectives simultaneously with limited interference, while remaining generalizable and adaptable across domains and offering flexible user control at inference time. Our code is available at: https://github.com/pearls-lab/multiobj-align."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01167","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:15Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00615","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00615","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00615","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minki","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Minki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei-Ning","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Wei-Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongge","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Dongge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huseyin A.","familyName":"Inan","name":"Inan, Huseyin A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Wutschitz","name":"Wutschitz, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yanzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Sim","name":"Sim, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saravan","familyName":"Rajmohan","name":"Rajmohan, Saravan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ACON: Optimizing Context Compression for Long-horizon LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:43:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:36:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-17T06:48:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-20T00:26:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-01T07:41:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:45:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are increasingly deployed as agents in dynamic real-world environments, where success depends on maintaining precise records of actions and observations. However, the resulting unbounded context growth in long-horizon agentic tasks makes two critical bottlenecks: prohibitive inference memory costs and reasoning degradation due to irrelevant information. Existing compression methods fail to fully address this, often relying on brittle heuristics or requiring parameter updates impractical for proprietary or large-scale LLMs. We introduce Agent Context Optimization (ACON), a unified framework that optimally compresses both observations and history into concise, informative representations. Distinct from prior works, ACON employs an optimization in natural language space: it iteratively refines compression guidelines based on failure analysis of the agent, ensuring critical state information is preserved without model fine-tuning. To further minimize computational overhead, we distill the optimized compressor into smaller models. Experiments on AppWorld, OfficeBench, and Multi-objective QA demonstrate that ACON reduces peak token usage by 26-54% while improving task success over existing compression baselines. Notably, it enables smaller LMs to function effectively as long-horizon agents, achieving up to 46% performance improvement by mitigating context distraction. Our code is available at https://github.com/microsoft/acon."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICML 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00615","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:12Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:04:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00481","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00481","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00481","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jiayang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangjie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Xiangjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zijie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antariksh","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Antariksh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zili","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Zili","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Make a Video Call with LLM: A Measurement Campaign over Six Mainstream Apps"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:03:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:26:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-30T15:21:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-02T00:43:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In 2025, Large Language Model (LLM) services have launched a new feature -- AI video chat -- allowing users to interact with AI agents via real-time video communication (RTC), just like chatting with real people. Despite its significance, no systematic study has characterized the performance of existing AI video chat systems. To address this gap, this paper proposes a comprehensive benchmark across four dimensions: quality, latency, internal mechanisms, and system overhead. Using custom testbeds, we further evaluate six mainstream AI video chatbots with this benchmark. We also build an online platform for user study. The measurement leads to interesting findings that could be beneficial to the future optimizations. For example, the network latency of AI video chat matters not as much as human video chat. The capabilities of AI agents matters most in the user experience. Our benchmarking results also open up several research questions for future optimizations of AI video chatbots. Availability: https://callarena.net/ for the online evaluation platform and our open-sourced dataset and testbed."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00481","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:00:47Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:00:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-02T04:53:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00948","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00948","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00948","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqing","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ziqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Xi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yucong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingnan","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Bingnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linghe","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Linghe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yulun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yulun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"InfVSR: Toward Consistency-Driven Streaming Generative Video Super-Resolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:21:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:57:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-21T11:36:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-22T00:50:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-22T02:38:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-25T00:18:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Real-world videos often extend over thousands of frames. Existing generative video super-resolution (VSR) approaches, however, face two persistent challenges when processing long sequences: (1) inefficiency due to the heavy cost of multi-step denoising for full-length sequences; and (2) poor consistency is hindered by temporal decomposition that causes artifacts and discontinuities. To break these limits, we propose InfVSR, which reformulates VSR as an autoregressive-one-step-diffusion paradigm, and enables streaming inference with video diffusion priors. First, we adapt the pretrained DiT into a causal structure, maintaining both local and global coherence via rolling KV-cache and joint visual guidance. Second, we distill the diffusion process into a single step efficiently, with patch-wise pixel supervision and cross-chunk distribution matching. To fill the gap in long-form video evaluation, we build a new benchmark tailored for extended sequences and further introduce semantic-level metrics to comprehensively assess temporal consistency. Our method pushes the frontier of long-form VSR, achieves state-of-the-art quality with enhanced semantic consistency, and delivers up to 58x speed-up over existing methods such as MGLD-VSR. Our code and models are available at https://github.com/Kai-Liu001/InfVSR."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code and model are available at https://github.com/Kai-Liu001/InfVSR"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00948","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:12:49Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:12:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-25T02:09:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00600","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00600","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00600","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pietro","familyName":"Mazzaglia","name":"Mazzaglia, Pietro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cansu","familyName":"Sancaktar","name":"Sancaktar, Cansu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Peschl","name":"Peschl, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Dijkman","name":"Dijkman, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid Training for Vision-Language-Action Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T07:27:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:35:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-19T06:34:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-20T00:38:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Using Large Language Models to produce intermediate thoughts, a.k.a. Chain-of-thought (CoT), before providing an answer has been a successful recipe for solving complex language tasks. In robotics, similar embodied CoT strategies, generating thoughts before actions, have also been shown to lead to improved performance when using Vision-Language-Action models (VLAs). As these techniques increase the length of the model's generated outputs to include the thoughts, the inference time is negatively affected. Delaying an agent's actions in real-world executions, as in robotic manipulation settings, strongly affects the usability of a method, as tasks require long sequences of actions. However, is the generation of long chains-of-thought a strong prerequisite for achieving performance improvements? In this work, we explore the idea of Hybrid Training (HyT), a framework that enables VLAs to learn from thoughts and benefit from the associated performance gains, while enabling the possibility to leave out CoT generation during inference. Furthermore, by learning to conditionally predict a diverse set of outputs, HyT supports flexibility at inference time, enabling the model to either predict actions directly, generate thoughts or follow instructions. We evaluate the proposed method in a series of simulated benchmarks and real-world experiments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00600","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:03:50Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:03:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-20T02:45:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jennifer","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Jennifer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Feature Identification via the Empirical NTK"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T03:39:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:24:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T17:53:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T01:13:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-06T14:33:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-09T01:45:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-06T17:51:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-07T01:04:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We provide evidence that eigenanalysis of the empirical neural tangent kernel (eNTK) can surface feature directions in trained neural networks. Across three increasingly realistic settings -- a 1-layer MLP trained on modular addition, a 1-layer Transformer trained on modular addition and the pretrained language model Gemma-3-270M -- we show that top eigenspaces of the eNTK align with ground-truth or interpretable features. In the modular arithmetic examples, top eNTK eigenspaces align with the Fourier features used by the MLP and the Fourier features at seed-dependent frequencies used by the Transformer to implement known ground-truth algorithms. Moreover, the alignment of the relevant subspaces evolves over training, with its first derivative peaking near the onset of grokking. For Gemma-3-270M, we compute top eNTK eigendirections on a dataset of TinyStories context windows and check their alignment with an automatically-generated set of parts-of-speech and other grammatical feature directions. We find that the alignment of eNTK eigendirections with grammar features outperforms a same-budget baseline of PCA on model activations. These results suggest that eNTK eigenanalysis may provide a new handle towards identifying features in trained models for mechanistic interpretability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 4 figures. v2: references and expanded discussion in Appendix B added. v3: Transformer case study and more appendices added. v4: Language model case study added"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:00:27Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:00:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-07T02:18:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicheng","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Yicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihua","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chongyu","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Chongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Changsheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Changsheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghan","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Jinghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sijia","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Sijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Downgrade to Upgrade: Optimizer Simplification Enhances Robustness in LLM Unlearning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T10:50:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:46:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T06:40:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:28:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T12:38:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-14T01:53:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-05T00:28:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:12:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-18T07:30:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-04-21T00:34:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model (LLM) unlearning aims to surgically remove the influence of undesired data or knowledge from an existing model while preserving its utility on unrelated tasks. This paradigm has shown promise in addressing privacy and safety concerns. However, recent findings reveal that unlearning effects are often fragile: post-unlearning manipulations such as weight quantization or fine-tuning can quickly neutralize the intended forgetting. Prior efforts to improve robustness primarily reformulate unlearning objectives by explicitly assuming the role of vulnerability sources. In this work, we take a different perspective by investigating the role of the optimizer, independent of unlearning objectives and formulations, in shaping unlearning robustness. We show that the 'grade' of the optimizer, defined by the level of information it exploits, ranging from zeroth-order (gradient-free) to first-order (gradient-based) to second-order (Hessian-based), is tightly linked to the resilience of unlearning. Surprisingly, we find that downgrading the optimizer, such as using zeroth-order methods or compressed-gradient variants (e.g., gradient sign-based optimizers), often leads to stronger robustness. While these optimizers produce noisier and less precise updates, they encourage convergence to harder-to-disturb basins in the loss landscape, thereby resisting post-training perturbations. By connecting zeroth-order methods with randomized smoothing, we further highlight their natural advantage for robust unlearning. Motivated by these insights, we propose a hybrid optimizer that combines first-order and zeroth-order updates, preserving unlearning efficacy while enhancing robustness. Extensive experiments on the MUSE and WMDP benchmarks, across multiple LLM unlearning algorithms, validate that our approach achieves more resilient forgetting without sacrificing unlearning quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:08:13Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:08:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-21T04:49:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00573","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00573","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00573","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yen-Ling","familyName":"Tai","name":"Tai, Yen-Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Ru","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yi-Ru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuan-Ting","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Kuan-Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Wei","familyName":"Chao","name":"Chao, Yu-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Ting","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yi-Ting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GRITS: A Spillage-Aware Guided Diffusion Policy for Robot Food Scooping Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T06:52:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:33:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-15T06:40:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-16T00:31:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Robotic food scooping is a critical manipulation skill for food preparation and service robots. However, existing robot learning algorithms, especially learn-from-demonstration methods, still struggle to handle diverse and dynamic food states, which often results in spillage and reduced reliability. In this work, we introduce GRITS: A Spillage-Aware Guided Diffusion Policy for Robot Food Scooping Tasks. This framework leverages guided diffusion policy to minimize food spillage during scooping and to ensure reliable transfer of food items from the initial to the target location. Specifically, we design a spillage predictor that estimates the probability of spillage given current observation and action rollout. The predictor is trained on a simulated dataset with food spillage scenarios, constructed from four primitive shapes (spheres, cubes, cones, and cylinders) with varied physical properties such as mass, friction, and particle size. At inference time, the predictor serves as a differentiable guidance signal, steering the diffusion sampling process toward safer trajectories while preserving task success. We validate GRITS on a real-world robotic food scooping platform. GRITS is trained on six food categories and evaluated on ten unseen categories with different shapes and quantities. GRITS achieves an 82% task success rate and a 4% spillage rate, reducing spillage by over 40% compared to baselines without guidance, thereby demonstrating its effectiveness. More details are available on our project website: https://hcis-lab.github.io/GRITS/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00573","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:03:10Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:03:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-16T02:18:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00766","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00766","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00766","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunki","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Eunki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Na Min","familyName":"An","name":"An, Na Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wan Ju","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Wan Ju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangryul","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Sangryul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Thorne","name":"Thorne, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunjung","familyName":"Shim","name":"Shim, Hyunjung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Are Large Vision-Language Models Ready to Guide Blind and Low-Vision Individuals?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T10:55:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:46:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-01T10:51:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) demonstrate a promising direction for assisting individuals with blindness or low-vision (BLV). Yet, measuring their true utility in real-world scenarios is challenging because evaluating whether their descriptions are BLV-informative requires a fundamentally different approach from assessing standard scene descriptions. While the \"VLM-as-a-metric\" or \"LVLM-as-a-judge\" paradigm has emerged, existing evaluators still fall short of capturing the unique requirements of BLV-centric evaluation, lacking at least one of the following key properties: (1) High correlation with human judgments, (2) Long instruction understanding, (3) Score generation efficiency, and (4) Multi-dimensional assessment. To this end, we propose a unified framework to bridge the gap between automated evaluation and actual BLV needs. First, we conduct an in-depth user study with BLV participants to understand and quantify their navigational preferences, curating VL-GUIDEDATA, a large-scale BLV user-simulated preference dataset containing image-request-response-score pairs. We then leverage the dataset to develop an accessibility-aware evaluator, VL-GUIDE-S, which outperforms existing (L)VLM judges in both human alignment and inference efficiency. Notably, its effectiveness extends beyond a single domain, demonstrating strong performance across multiple fine-grained, BLV-critical dimensions. We hope our work lays as a foundation for automatic AI judges that advance safe, barrier-free navigation for BLV users."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"42 pages, 14 figures, 28 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00766","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:08:20Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:08:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-02T02:28:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00430","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00430","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00430","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suhyeon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Suhyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jong Chul","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jong Chul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PromptLoop: Plug-and-Play Prompt Refinement via Latent Feedback for Diffusion Model Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T02:18:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:21:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-25T08:43:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:38:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite recent progress, reinforcement learning (RL)-based fine-tuning of diffusion models often struggles with generalization, composability, and robustness against reward hacking. Recent studies have explored prompt refinement as a modular alternative, but most adopt a feed-forward approach that applies a single refined prompt throughout the entire sampling trajectory, thereby failing to fully leverage the sequential nature of reinforcement learning. To address this, we introduce PromptLoop, a plug-and-play RL framework that incorporates latent feedback into step-wise prompt refinement. Rather than modifying diffusion model weights, a multimodal large language model (MLLM) is trained with RL to iteratively update prompts based on intermediate latent states of diffusion models. This design achieves a structural analogy to the Diffusion RL approach, while retaining the flexibility and generality of prompt-based alignment. Extensive experiments across diverse reward functions and diffusion backbones demonstrate that PromptLoop (i) achieves effective reward optimization, (ii) generalizes seamlessly to unseen models, (iii) composes orthogonally with existing alignment methods, and (iv) mitigates over-optimization and reward hacking while introducing only a practically negligible inference overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"CVPR26 poster. 25 pages, 19 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00430","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:59:29Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:59:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:29:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00379","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00379","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00379","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bilge","familyName":"Acun","name":"Acun, Bilge","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prasoon","familyName":"Sinha","name":"Sinha, Prasoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Newsha","familyName":"Ardalani","name":"Ardalani, Newsha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangmin","familyName":"Bae","name":"Bae, Sangmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alicia","familyName":"Golden","name":"Golden, Alicia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Yu","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Chien-Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meghana","familyName":"Madhyastha","name":"Madhyastha, Meghana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neeraja J.","familyName":"Yadwadkar","name":"Yadwadkar, Neeraja J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carole-Jean","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Carole-Jean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Composer: A Search Framework for Hybrid Neural Architecture Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T00:51:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:16:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-10T18:59:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-12T00:06:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hybrid model architectures that combine computational primitives (e.g., Attention, MLP) in different ratios have shown promising performance beyond Transformers. Some studies have shown that different interleavings of primitives can affect model quality as well. However, prior works explore the hybrid model architecture design space manually. Due to the large design space and training costs, discovering hybrid models that combine key computational primitives for pre-training is challenging. In this work, we take a principled approach in designing a modular hybrid model architecture search framework -- Composer. Composer explores model architectures at a small scale and extrapolates the top-performing model architectures to a larger scale using our proposed scaling strategies. Using Composer, we discover new hybrid LLM architectures that outperform Llama 3.2. Compared to Llama 3.2 and previous state-of-the-art baselines, the new model architectures consistently reduce validation loss at parameter scales of 350M-3B and improve evaluation accuracy on the downstream tasks by up to 2.8-8.3% (1.1-3.1% on average) while improving both training and inference efficiency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00379","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:15Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-12T02:25:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00685","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00685","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00685","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nurbek","familyName":"Tastan","name":"Tastan, Nurbek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Horvath","name":"Horvath, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Nandakumar","name":"Nandakumar, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stochastic Self-Organization in Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T09:08:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:41:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-09T00:21:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T01:31:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent systems (MAS) based on Large Language Models (LLMs) have the potential to solve tasks that are beyond the reach of any single LLM. However, this potential can only be realized when the collaboration mechanism between agents is optimized. Specifically, optimizing the communication structure between agents is critical for fruitful collaboration. Most existing approaches rely on fixed topologies, pretrained graph generators, optimization over edges, or employ external LLM judges, thereby adding to the complexity. In this work, we introduce a response-conditioned framework that adapts communication on-the-fly. Agents independently generate responses to the user query and assess peer contributions using an approximation of the Shapley value. A directed acyclic graph (DAG) is then constructed to regulate the propagation of the responses among agents, which ensures stable and efficient message transmission from high-contributing agents to others. This graph is dynamically updated based on the agent responses from the previous collaboration round. Since the proposed framework enables the self-organization of agents without additional supervision or training, we refer to it as SelfOrg. The SelfOrg framework goes beyond task- and query-level optimization and takes into account the stochastic nature of agent responses. Experiments with both strong and weak LLM backends demonstrate robust performance, with significant gains in the weak regime where prior methods collapse. We also theoretically show that multiple agents increase the chance of correctness and that the correct responses naturally dominate the information flow."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00685","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:06:12Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:06:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:50:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00857","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00857","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00857","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adi","familyName":"Simhi","name":"Simhi, Adi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Herzig","name":"Herzig, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Tutek","name":"Tutek, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Itay","familyName":"Itzhak","name":"Itzhak, Itay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Idan","familyName":"Szpektor","name":"Szpektor, Idan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonatan","familyName":"Belinkov","name":"Belinkov, Yonatan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ManagerBench: Evaluating the Safety-Pragmatism Trade-off in Autonomous LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:52:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T10:31:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:42:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) evolve from conversational assistants into autonomous agents, evaluating the safety of their actions becomes critical. Prior safety benchmarks have primarily focused on preventing generation of harmful content, such as toxic text. However, they overlook the challenge of agents taking harmful actions when the most effective path to an operational goal conflicts with human safety. To address this gap, we introduce ManagerBench, a benchmark that evaluates LLM decision-making in realistic, human-validated managerial scenarios. Each scenario forces a choice between a pragmatic but harmful action that achieves an operational goal, and a safe action that leads to worse operational performance. A parallel control set, where potential harm is directed only at inanimate objects, measures a model's pragmatism and identifies its tendency to be overly safe. Our findings indicate that the frontier LLMs perform poorly when navigating this safety-pragmatism trade-off. Many consistently choose harmful options to advance their operational goals, while others avoid harm only to become overly safe and ineffective. Critically, we find this misalignment does not stem from an inability to perceive harm, as models' harm assessments align with human judgments, but from flawed prioritization. ManagerBench is a challenging benchmark for a core component of agentic behavior: making safe choices when operational goals and alignment values incentivize conflicting actions. Benchmark \u0026amp; code available at https://technion-cs-nlp.github.io/ManagerBench-website/."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00857","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:10:36Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:10:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00981","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00981","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00981","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leying","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Leying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xiaofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heng","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Heng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manthan","familyName":"Thakker","name":"Thakker, Manthan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FlexiCodec: A Dynamic Neural Audio Codec for Low Frame Rates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:56:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:59:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T02:19:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:20:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-25T08:24:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:34:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Neural audio codecs are foundational to speech language models. It is expected to have a low frame rate and decoupled semantic and acoustic information. A lower frame rate codec can reduce the computational cost of speech language models by shortening the sequence length. Recent studies have developed 12.5Hz low-frame-rate audio codecs, but even lower frame rate codecs remain underexplored. We find that a major challenge for very low frame rate tokens is missing semantic information. This paper introduces FlexiCodec to address this limitation. FlexiCodec improves semantic preservation with a dynamic frame rate approach and introduces a novel architecture featuring an ASR feature-assisted dual stream encoding and Transformer bottlenecks. With dynamic frame rates, it uses less frames at information-sparse regions through adaptively merging semantically similar frames. A dynamic frame rate also allows FlexiCodec to support inference-time controllable frame rates between 3Hz and 12.5Hz. Experiments on 6.25Hz, 8.3Hz and 12.5Hz average frame rates confirm that FlexiCodec excels over baseline systems in semantic information preservation and delivers a high audio reconstruction quality. We also validate the effectiveness of FlexiCodec in language model-based TTS. Demos are available at: https://flexicodec.github.io. Code is available at: https://github.com/amphionteam/flexicodec."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00981","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:13:38Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:13:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:26:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Hossein","familyName":"Samaei","name":"Samaei, Mohammad Hossein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faryad Darabi","familyName":"Sahneh","name":"Sahneh, Faryad Darabi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lee W.","familyName":"Cohnstaedt","name":"Cohnstaedt, Lee W.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caterina","familyName":"Scoglio","name":"Scoglio, Caterina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EpidemIQs: Prompt-to-Paper LLM Agents for Epidemic Modeling and Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Social and Information Networks (cs.SI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-24T18:54:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-25T09:22:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-26T01:37:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) offer new opportunities to accelerate complex interdisciplinary research domains. Epidemic modeling, characterized by its complexity and reliance on network science, dynamical systems, epidemiology, and stochastic simulations, represents a prime candidate for leveraging LLM-driven automation. We introduce EpidemIQs, a novel multi-agent LLM framework that integrates user inputs and autonomously conducts literature review, analytical derivation, network modeling, mechanistic modeling, stochastic simulations, data visualization and analysis, and finally documentation of findings in a structured manuscript, through five predefined research phases. We introduce two types of agents: a scientist agent for planning, coordination, reflection, and generation of final results, and a task-expert agent to focus exclusively on one specific duty serving as a tool to the scientist agent. The framework consistently generated complete reports in scientific article format. Specifically, using GPT 4.1 and GPT 4.1 Mini as backbone LLMs for scientist and task-expert agents, respectively, the autonomous process completes with average total token usage 870K at a cost of about $1.57 per study, successfully executing all phases and final report. We evaluate EpidemIQs across several different epidemic scenarios, measuring computational cost, workflow reliability, task success rate, and LLM-as-Judge and human expert reviews to estimate the overall quality and technical correctness of the generated results. Through our experiments, the framework consistently addresses evaluation scenarios with an average task success rate of 79%. We compare EpidemIQs to an iterative single-agent LLM, benefiting from the same system prompts and tools, iteratively planning, invoking tools, and revising outputs until task completion. The comparisons suggest a consistently higher performance of EpidemIQs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:34Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-26T03:26:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00565","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00565","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00565","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shojiro","familyName":"Yamabe","name":"Yamabe, Shojiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Sakuma","name":"Sakuma, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward Safer Diffusion Language Models: Discovery and Mitigation of Priming Vulnerability"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T06:35:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:32:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-17T05:41:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-18T01:21:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion language models (DLMs) generate tokens in parallel through iterative denoising, which can reduce latency and enable bidirectional conditioning. However, the safety risks posed by jailbreak attacks that exploit this inference mechanism are not well understood. In this paper, we reveal that DLMs have a critical vulnerability stemming from their iterative denoising process and propose a countermeasure. Specifically, our investigation shows that if an affirmative token for a harmful query appears at an intermediate step, subsequent denoising can be steered toward a harmful response even in aligned models. As a result, simply injecting such affirmative tokens can readily bypass the safety guardrails. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the vulnerability allows existing optimization-based jailbreak attacks to succeed on DLMs. Building on this analysis, we propose a novel safety alignment method tailored to DLMs that trains models to generate safe responses from contaminated intermediate states that contain affirmative tokens. Our experiments indicate that the proposed method significantly mitigates the vulnerability with minimal impact on task performance. Furthermore, our method improves robustness against conventional jailbreak attacks. Our work underscores the need for DLM-specific safety research. Our code is available at https://github.com/mdl-lab/dlm-priming-vulnerability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00565","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:02:58Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:02:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-18T02:57:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikas","familyName":"Dwivedi","name":"Dwivedi, Vikas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrico","familyName":"Schiassi","name":"Schiassi, Enrico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monica","familyName":"Sigovan","name":"Sigovan, Monica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruno","familyName":"Sixou","name":"Sixou, Bruno","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gated X-TFC: Soft Domain Decomposition for Forward and Inverse Problems in Sharp-Gradient PDEs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T15:43:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-17T01:44:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.neucom.2026.133090","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Physics-informed neural networks (PINNs) and related methods struggle to resolve sharp gradients in singularly perturbed boundary value problems without resorting to some form of domain decomposition, which often introduce complex interface penalties. While the Extreme Theory of Functional Connections (X-TFC) avoids multi-objective optimization by employing exact boundary condition enforcement, it remains computationally inefficient for boundary layers and incompatible with decomposition. We propose Gated X-TFC, a novel framework for both forward and inverse problems, that overcomes these limitations through a soft, learned domain decomposition. Our method replaces hard interfaces with a differentiable logistic gate that dynamically adapts radial basis function (RBF) kernel widths across the domain, eliminating the need for interface penalties. This approach yields not only superior accuracy but also dramatic improvements in computational efficiency: on a benchmark one dimensional (1D) convection-diffusion, Gated X-TFC achieves an order-of-magnitude lower error than standard X-TFC while using 80 percent fewer collocation points and reducing training time by 66 percent. In addition, we introduce an operator-conditioned meta-learning layer that learns a probabilistic mapping from PDE parameters to optimal gate configurations, enabling fast, uncertainty-aware warm-starting for new problem instances. We further demonstrate scalability to multiple subdomains and higher dimensions by solving a twin boundary-layer equation and a 2D Poisson problem with a sharp Gaussian source. Overall, Gated X-TFC delivers a simple alternative alternative to PINNs that is both accurate and computationally efficient for challenging boundar-layer regimes. Future work will focus on nonlinear problems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T02:15:05Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:15:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-17T04:45:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun-ichiro","familyName":"Hayashi","name":"Hayashi, Shun-ichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koki","familyName":"Morita","name":"Morita, Koki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daichi","familyName":"Mukunoki","name":"Mukunoki, Daichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya","familyName":"Hoshino","name":"Hoshino, Tetsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takahiro","familyName":"Katagiri","name":"Katagiri, Takahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VibeCodeHPC: An Agent-Based Iterative Prompting Auto-Tuner for HPC Code Generation Using LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T04:54:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-05T02:24:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:17:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-12T14:08:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:52:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this study, we propose VibeCodeHPC, a multi-agent system based on large language models (LLMs) for the automatic tuning of high-performance computing (HPC) programs on supercomputers. VibeCodeHPC adopts Claude Code as its backend and provides an integrated environment that facilitates program development in supercomputer settings. The system not only brings the Vibe Coding paradigm -- program development through natural language interaction with users -- to HPC programming, but also enables autonomous performance optimization with minimal user intervention through a sophisticated multi-agent design. To achieve these objectives, VibeCodeHPC implements three core functionalities: (1) configuration capabilities tailored to the unique development environments of supercomputers, (2) collaborative operation among multiple LLM agents with distinct roles -- Project Manager (PM), System Engineer (SE), Programmer (PG), and Continuous Deliverer (CD), and (3) long-term autonomous operation through agent activity monitoring and dynamic deployment mechanisms. This paper highlights one of the most powerful features of VibeCodeHPC: fully automated code optimization through autonomous operation without user intervention. Specifically, it demonstrates the performance optimization of CPU-based codes on GPU-equipped systems for matrix multiplication and a Poisson equation solver using Jacobi's iterative method. The results show that the multi-agent configuration employed in VibeCodeHPC enables faster and more reliable development of higher-performance code compared to a single-agent setup."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:44Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00294","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00294","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00294","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shutong","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Shutong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Free Draft-and-Verification: Toward Lossless Parallel Decoding for Diffusion Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T21:28:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:12:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-01T23:45:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:44:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T03:37:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-04T01:28:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Large Language Models (DLLMs) have emerged as a new paradigm of language modeling beyond autoregressive next-token prediction. Taking advantage of their inherent modeling foundations, DLLMs have the great potential of efficient inference with parallel decoding algorithms, which enable multi-token prediction. However, the high generation quality often requires the number of decoding steps equal to the sequence length, which performs a one-token-per-step decoding, and existing parallel decoding algorithms, which yield suboptimal decoding paths, bring inference speedup at the cost of non-negligible performance degradation. To overcome this challenge, we introduce Free Draft-and-Verification (FreeDave), a novel fast decoding algorithm tailored for DLLMs that achieves lossless parallel decoding without any model modification or extra modules. Specifically, we propose an algorithm of parallel-decoded candidate generation and verification, which is theoretically guaranteed to use the fewest model forward calls to reproduce the same sequence generated by one-token-per-step decoding. By extensive evaluations on math reasoning and code generation benchmarks across different DLLMs, FreeDave is proven to accelerate the inference up to $2.83\\times$ without performance degradation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00294","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:14Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-04T03:41:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00219","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00219","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00219","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houjun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Houjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikhar","familyName":"Murty","name":"Murty, Shikhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher D.","familyName":"Manning","name":"Manning, Christopher D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Róbert","familyName":"Csordás","name":"Csordás, Róbert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thoughtbubbles: an Unsupervised Method for Parallel Thinking in Latent Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:49:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:08:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T08:56:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:37:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current approaches for scaling inference-time compute in transformers train them to emit explicit chain-of-thought tokens before producing an answer. While these methods are powerful, they are limited because they cannot be applied during pretraining and rely solely on serially-generated, natural-language verbalization. In this work, we propose Thoughtbubbles, a transformer variant that natively performs parallel adaptive computation in latent space by learning to fork or delete residual streams. Thus, tokens requiring more computation can form a \"bubble\" of cloned residuals in the middle of the network. Crucially, this behavior is learned during pretraining with only language modeling loss. Using half of the training budget, Thoughtbubbles outperforms the perplexity and zero-shot evals of both standard decoder LMs and those using non-adaptive parallel computation approaches. These results hold across model sizes from 150M to 1.9B. Thoughtbubbles achieves competitive GSM8K results using half of the baseline's token budget. The implicit nature of our method enables models to begin learning adaptive computation at pretraining time, paving the way to unified train-time and test-time scaling behaviors."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00219","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:24Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00065","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00065","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00065","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abdelrhman","familyName":"Gaber","name":"Gaber, Abdelrhman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad","familyName":"ElMahdy","name":"ElMahdy, Muhammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youssif","familyName":"Abuzied","name":"Abuzied, Youssif","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Abd-Eltawab","name":"Abd-Eltawab, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tamer","familyName":"ElBatt","name":"ElBatt, Tamer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FedLLM-Align: Feature Extraction From Heterogeneous Clients"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T14:06:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T03:36:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-29T19:35:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:03:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Federated learning (FL) enables collaborative model training without sharing raw data, making it attractive for privacy-sensitive domains, e.g., healthcare, finance, and IoT. A major obstacle, however, is the potential heterogeneity of tabular data across clients, in practical settings, where schema mismatches and incompatible feature spaces prevent straightforward aggregation. To address this challenge, this paper proposes FedLLM-Align, a federated learning framework that leverages pretrained transformer based language models for feature extraction. Towards this objective, FedLLM-Align serializes tabular records into text and derives semantically aligned embeddings from a pretrained LLM encoder, e.g, DistilBERT, facilitating lightweight local classifier heads that can be trained in a federated manner using standard aggregation schemes, e.g., FedAvg, while keeping all raw data records local. To quantify the merits and trade-offs of FedLLM-Align, we evaluate the proposed framework on binary classification tasks from two different domains: i) Coronary heart disease prediction on partitioned Framingham Heart Study data, and ii) Customer churn prediction on a financial dataset. FedLLM-Align outperforms state-of-the-art baselines by up to 25% in terms of the F1 score, under simulated schema heterogeneity, and achieves a 65% reduction in the communication overhead. These results establish FedLLM-Align as a privacy-preserving and communication-efficient approach for federated training based on clients with heterogeneous tabular datasets, commonly encountered in practice."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00065","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:35Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00666","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00666","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00666","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leah","familyName":"Bar","name":"Bar, Leah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liron Mor","familyName":"Yosef","name":"Yosef, Liron Mor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shai","familyName":"Zucker","name":"Zucker, Shai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Neta","familyName":"Shoham","name":"Shoham, Neta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Inbar","familyName":"Seroussi","name":"Seroussi, Inbar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nir","familyName":"Sochen","name":"Sochen, Nir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Geometric Unification of Generative AI with Manifold-Probabilistic Projection Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:50:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:40:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-15T13:08:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-16T01:42:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most models of generative AI for images assume that images are inherently low-dimensional objects embedded within a high-dimensional space. Additionally, it is often implicitly assumed that thematic image datasets form smooth or piecewise smooth manifolds. Common approaches overlook the geometric structure and focus solely on probabilistic methods, approximating the probability distribution through universal approximation techniques such as the kernel method. In some generative models the low dimensional nature of the data manifest itself by the introduction of a lower dimensional latent space. Yet, the probability distribution in the latent or the manifold's coordinate space is considered uninteresting and is predefined or considered uniform. In this study, we address the problem of Blind Image Denoising (BID), and to some extent, the problem of generating images from noise by unifying geometric and probabilistic perspectives. We introduce a novel framework that improves upon existing probabilistic approaches by incorporating geometric assumptions that enable the effective use of kernel-based probabilistic methods. Furthermore, the proposed framework extends prior geometric approaches by combining explicit and implicit manifold descriptions through the introduction of a distance function. The resulting framework demystifies diffusion models by interpreting them as a projection mechanism onto the manifold of ``good images''. This interpretation leads to the construction of a new deterministic model, the Manifold-Probabilistic Projection Model (MPPM), which operates in both the representation (pixel) space and the latent space. We demonstrate that the Latent MPPM (LMPPM) outperforms the Latent Diffusion Model (LDM) across various datasets, achieving superior results in terms of image restoration and generation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00666","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:05:35Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:05:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-16T03:10:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00940","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00940","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00940","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding Bang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Ding Bang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kuang Hong","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Kuang Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tie","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Tie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng Fan","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Meng Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi Yan","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Zhi Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao Xia","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiao Xia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qian Jin","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Qian Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi Qing","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhi Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Anisotropic linear magnetoresistance in nanoflakes of Dirac semimetal NiTe2"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Mesoscale and Nanoscale Physics (cond-mat.mes-hall)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:16:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:57:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-06T23:31:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-09T01:39:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work investigates the magneto-transport properties of exfoliated NiTe2 nano-flakes with varying thicknesses and disorder levels, unveiling two distinct physical mechanisms governing the observed anisotropic linear magnetoresistance (MR). For the perpendicular magnetic field configuration, the well-defined linear MR in high fields is unambiguously attributed to a classical origin. This conclusion is supported by the proportionality between the MR slope and the carrier mobility, and between the crossover field and the inverse of mobility. In stark contrast, the linear MR under parallel magnetic fields exhibits a non-classical character. It shows a pronounced enhancement with decreasing flake thickness, which correlates with an increasing hole-to-electron concentration ratio. This distinctive thickness dependence suggests an origin in the nonlinear band effects near the Dirac point, likely driven by the shift of the Fermi level. Furthermore, the strengthening of MR anisotropic with enhanced inter-layer transport contradicts the prediction of the guiding-center diffusion model for three-dimensional systems. Our findings highlight the critical roles of band topology and structural dimensional in the anomalous magneto-transport of Dirac semi-metals."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00940","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:12:36Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:12:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-09T04:36:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00229","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00229","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00229","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rohan","familyName":"Kadekodi","name":"Kadekodi, Rohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhan","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keisuke","familyName":"Kamahori","name":"Kamahori, Keisuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yile","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Yile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Khatiri","name":"Khatiri, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah H.","familyName":"Bayindirli","name":"Bayindirli, Noah H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Gorbunov","name":"Gorbunov, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baris","familyName":"Kasikci","name":"Kasikci, Baris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentFlux: Decoupled Fine-Tuning \u0026amp; Inference for On-Device Agentic Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:52:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:08:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-19T05:16:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:45:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-11T02:12:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-12T01:15:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-12T04:25:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:22:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) as agentic orchestrators has revolutionized task automation, but the need for privacy-preserving, cost-effective solutions demands on-device inference capabilities. However, local LLMs consistently underperform compared to frontier models in tool calling scenarios, struggling with both tool selection from large tool sets and accurate argument generation for complex parameter structures. We introduce a methodology that disaggregates a tool-calling task into two distinct subtasks: tool selection and argument generation. We propose \"decoupled fine-tuning\", a novel post-training approach that employs LoRA fine-tuning to create dedicated LoRA adapters for tool selection and tool-specific argument generation using separate loss masking for each of the subtasks. Furthermore, we present AgentFlux, an inference framework that leverages the LoRA adapters created using decoupled fine-tuning to perform efficient agent orchestration with the help of local models on end-user devices. AgentFlux decomposes the tool-call generation step into tool selection and argument generation, and dynamically loads the corresponding LoRA adapters to generate tool calls. Additionally, AgentFlux implements hierarchical orchestration to restrict the number of tools required for tool selection. Our experiments on the MCP-Bench benchmark demonstrate that the Qwen-2.5-7B model trained using decoupled fine-tuning improves the tool calling accuracy of the base model by 46%, and outperforms other local reasoning, non-reasoning and fine-tuned models of similar size in all cases, and models that are 2x larger, in most cases."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00229","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:39Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-13T03:09:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01176","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01176","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01176","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiye","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Jiye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenghui","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chenghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linh","familyName":"Tran","name":"Tran, Linh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shih-En","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Shih-En","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason","familyName":"Saragih","name":"Saragih, Jason","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Richard","name":"Richard, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanbyul","familyName":"Joo","name":"Joo, Hanbyul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaojie","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Shaojie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Audio Driven Real-Time Facial Animation for Social Telepresence"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:57:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:11:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-01T15:10:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:35:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3757377.3763854","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present an audio-driven real-time system for animating photorealistic 3D facial avatars with minimal latency, designed for social interactions in virtual reality for anyone. Central to our approach is an encoder model that transforms audio signals into latent facial expression sequences in real time, which are then decoded as photorealistic 3D facial avatars. Leveraging the generative capabilities of diffusion models, we capture the rich spectrum of facial expressions necessary for natural communication while achieving real-time performance (\u0026lt;15ms GPU time). Our novel architecture minimizes latency through two key innovations: an online transformer that eliminates dependency on future inputs and a distillation pipeline that accelerates iterative denoising into a single step. We further address critical design challenges in live scenarios for processing continuous audio signals frame-by-frame while maintaining consistent animation quality. The versatility of our framework extends to multimodal applications, including semantic modalities such as emotion conditions and multimodal sensors with head-mounted eye cameras on VR headsets. Experimental results demonstrate significant improvements in facial animation accuracy over existing offline state-of-the-art baselines, achieving 100 to 1000 times faster inference speed. We validate our approach through live VR demonstrations and across various scenarios such as multilingual speeches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"SIGGRAPH Asia 2025. Project page: https://jiyewise.github.io/projects/AudioRTA"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01176","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T02:18:29Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:18:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:57:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00395","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00395","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00395","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaye","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Jiaye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haonan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Haonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaiqi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shuaiqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yishan","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Yishan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zian","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Zian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Roujia","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Roujia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoran","familyName":"Cheng","name":"Cheng, Haoran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Q. Vera","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Q. Vera","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao-Wen","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Hao-Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SAGE-Music: Low-Latency Symbolic Music Generation via Attribute-Specialized Key-Value Head Sharing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T01:11:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:17:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-15T02:29:01Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v2; Withdrawn after identifying that results in Section 5 require additional re-analysis before public dissemination"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:19:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-latency symbolic music generation is essential for real-time improvisation and human-AI co-creation. Existing transformer-based models, however, face a trade-off between inference speed and musical quality. Traditional acceleration techniques such as embedding pooling significantly degrade quality, while recently proposed Byte Pair Encoding (BPE) methods - though effective on single-track piano data - suffer large performance drops in multi-track settings, as revealed by our analysis. We propose Attribute-Specialized Key-Value Head Sharing (AS-KVHS), adapted to music's structured symbolic representation, achieving about 30% inference speedup with only a negligible (about 0.4%) quality drop in objective evaluations and slight improvements in subjective listening tests. Our main contributions are (1) the first systematic study of BPE's generalizability in multi-track symbolic music, and (2) the introduction of AS-KVHS for low-latency symbolic music generation. Beyond these, we also release SAGE-Music, an open-source benchmark that matches or surpasses state-of-the-art models in generation quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Withdrawn after identifying that results in Section 5 require additional re-analysis before public dissemination"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00395","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:39Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00133","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00133","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00133","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adarsha","familyName":"Balaji","name":"Balaji, Adarsha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep","familyName":"Madireddy","name":"Madireddy, Sandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prasanna","familyName":"Balaprakash","name":"Balaprakash, Prasanna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Inference Engines based on Spiking Neural Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:11:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:03:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T14:15:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-03T00:49:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-14T22:02:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-16T00:11:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundational models based on the transformer architecture are currently the state-of-the-art in general language modeling, as well as in scientific areas such as material science and climate. However, training and deploying these models is computationally challenging as the time and space complexity has a quadratic relation to the input sequence length. Several efforts exploring efficient computational paradigms and model architectures to address these limitations have been made. In this work, we explore spiking neural networks (SNNs) to design transformer models. A challenge in training large-scale SNNs, using existing surrogate learning methods is inefficient and time-consuming. On the other hand, techniques to convert existing transformer-based models to their SNN equivalent are not scalable, as achieving optimal performance comes at the cost of a large number of spike time-steps, i.e. increased latency. To address this, we propose NeurTransformer, a methodology for designing transformer-based SNN for inference using a supervised fine-tuning approach with existing conversion methods. The proposed methodology works by: (1) replacing the self-attention mechanism with a spike-based self-attention (SSA), (2) converting the feed-forward block of the trained transformer model to its equivalent SNN, and (3) fine-tuning the SSA block using SNN-based surrogate learning algorithms. We benchmark the proposed methodology and demonstrate its accuracy and scalability using three variants of the GPT-2 model of increasing model size. We observe that the converted GPT-2 small models demonstrate a 5-12% loss in cosine similarity and a 9.7% reduction in perplexity. Finally, we demonstrate the energy efficiency of the SSA block compared to the ASA block and show between 64.71% and 85.28% reductions in estimated energy consumption when implementing the self-attention mechanism on a digital hardware."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00133","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:52:15Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:52:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-16T02:26:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongqi","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Dongqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ARS: Adaptive Reasoning Suppression for Efficient Large Reasoning Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-29T20:19:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:01:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T15:04:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:55:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Reasoning Language Models (LRLMs or LRMs) demonstrate remarkable capabilities in complex reasoning tasks, but suffer from significant computational inefficiencies due to overthinking phenomena. Existing efficient reasoning methods face the challenge of balancing reasoning quality with inference cost reduction. We propose \\textbf{Adaptive Reasoning Suppression (ARS)}, a novel training-free approach that dynamically suppresses redundant reasoning steps while preserving accuracy through adaptive certainty monitoring. ARS introduces a multi-checkpoint certainty estimation mechanism with progressive suppression thresholds, achieving superior efficiency compared to static suppression methods. Our extensive evaluation across mathematical reasoning benchmarks using multiple model architectures demonstrates that ARS achieves up to 53%, 46.1%, and 57.9% in token, latency and energy reduction, while maintaining or improving accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by 39th NeurIPS - Foundations of Reasoning in Language Models"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:50:44Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:50:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:16:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00207","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00207","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00207","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunqi","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yunqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bing","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Bing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi Boloursaz","familyName":"Mashhadi","name":"Mashhadi, Mahdi Boloursaz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A-Long","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, A-Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Pei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rahim","familyName":"Tafazolli","name":"Tafazolli, Rahim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Merouane","familyName":"Debbah","name":"Debbah, Merouane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FlowMoE: A Scalable Pipeline Scheduling Framework for Distributed Mixture-of-Experts Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:31:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:07:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T15:54:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:56:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The parameter size of modern large language models (LLMs) can be scaled up via the sparsely-activated Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) technique to avoid excessive increase of the computational costs. To further improve training efficiency, pipelining computation and communication has become a promising solution for distributed MoE training. However, existing work primarily focuses on scheduling tasks within the MoE layer, such as expert computing and all-to-all (A2A) communication, while neglecting other key operations including multi-head attention (MHA) computing, gating, and all-reduce communication. In this paper, we propose FlowMoE, a scalable framework for scheduling multi-type task pipelines. First, FlowMoE constructs a unified pipeline to consistently scheduling MHA computing, gating, expert computing, and A2A communication. Second, FlowMoE introduces a tensor chunk-based priority scheduling mechanism to overlap the all-reduce communication with all computing tasks. We implement FlowMoE as an adaptive and generic framework atop PyTorch. Extensive experiments with 675 typical MoE layers and four real-world MoE models across two GPU clusters demonstrate that our proposed FlowMoE framework outperforms state-of-the-art MoE training frameworks, reducing training time by 13%-57%, energy consumption by 10%-39%, and memory usage by 7%-32%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00207","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:05Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:26:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01126","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01126","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01126","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Keyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassane","familyName":"Ouchouid","name":"Ouchouid, Hassane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashwil","familyName":"Kaniamparambil","name":"Kaniamparambil, Ashwil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ioannis","familyName":"Souflas","name":"Souflas, Ioannis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panagiotis","familyName":"Angeloudis","name":"Angeloudis, Panagiotis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Strategic Fusion of Vision Language Models: Shapley-Credited Context-Aware Dawid-Skene for Multi-Label Tasks in Autonomous Driving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T17:14:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:08:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large vision-language models (VLMs) are increasingly used in autonomous-vehicle (AV) stacks, but hallucination limits their reliability in safety-critical pipelines. We present Shapley-credited Context-Aware Dawid-Skene with Agreement, a game-theoretic fusion method for multi-label understanding of ego-view dashcam video. It learns per-model, per-label, context-conditioned reliabilities from labelled history and, at inference, converts each model's report into an agreement-guardrailed log-likelihood ratio that is combined with a contextual prior and a public reputation state updated via Shapley-based team credit. The result is calibrated, thresholdable posteriors that (i) amplify agreement among reliable models, (ii) preserve uniquely correct single-model signals, and (iii) adapt to drift. To specialise general VLMs, we curate 1,000 real-world dashcam clips with structured annotations (scene description, manoeuvre recommendation, rationale) via an automatic pipeline that fuses HDD ground truth, vehicle kinematics, and YOLOv11 + BoT-SORT tracking, guided by a three-step chain-of-thought prompt; three heterogeneous VLMs are then fine-tuned with LoRA. We evaluate with Hamming distance, Micro-Macro-F1, and average per-video latency. Empirically, the proposed method achieves a 23% reduction in Hamming distance, 55% improvement in Macro-F1, and 47% improvement in Micro-F1 when comparing with the best single model, supporting VLM fusion as a calibrated, interpretable, and robust decision-support component for AV pipelines."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01126","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:17:15Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:17:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:17:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01069","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.01069","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.01069","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elija","familyName":"Perrier","name":"Perrier, Elija","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Typed Chain-of-Thought: A Curry-Howard Framework for Verifying LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T16:06:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T01:05:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting enhances the reasoning capabilities of large language models, the faithfulness of the generated rationales remains an open problem for model interpretability. We propose a novel theoretical lens for this problem grounded in the Curry-Howard correspondence, which posits a direct relationship between formal proofs and computer programs. Under this paradigm, a faithful reasoning trace is analogous to a well-typed program, where each intermediate step corresponds to a typed logical inference. We operationalise this analogy, presenting methods to extract and map the informal, natural language steps of CoT into a formal, typed proof structure. Successfully converting a CoT trace into a well-typed proof serves as a strong, verifiable certificate of its computational faithfulness, moving beyond heuristic interpretability towards formal verification. Our framework provides a methodology to transform plausible narrative explanations into formally verifiable programs, offering a path towards building more reliable and trustworthy AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.01069","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:15:50Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:15:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:15:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00974","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00974","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00974","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siheng","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Siheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengtao","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Zhengtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengdao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhengdao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junhao","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Junhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanshu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yikai","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linshan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Linshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yijiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhikang","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zhikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huacan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huacan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jifeng","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"JEPA-T: Joint-Embedding Predictive Architecture with Text Fusion for Image Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:51:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:59:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern Text-to-Image (T2I) generation increasingly relies on token-centric architectures that are trained with self-supervision, yet effectively fusing text with visual tokens remains a challenge. We propose \\textbf{JEPA-T}, a unified multimodal framework that encodes images and captions into discrete visual and textual tokens, processed by a joint-embedding predictive Transformer. To enhance fusion, we incorporate cross-attention after the feature predictor for conditional denoising while maintaining a task-agnostic backbone. Additionally, raw texts embeddings are injected prior to the flow matching loss to improve alignment during training. During inference, the same network performs both class-conditional and free-text image generation by iteratively denoising visual tokens conditioned on text. Evaluations on ImageNet-1K demonstrate that JEPA-T achieves strong data efficiency, open-vocabulary generalization, and consistently outperforms non-fusion and late-fusion baselines. Our approach shows that late architectural fusion combined with objective-level alignment offers an effective balance between conditioning strength and backbone generality in token-based T2I.The code is now available: https://github.com/justin-herry/JEPA-T.git"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00974","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:13:27Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:13:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:13:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00960","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00960","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00960","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miha","familyName":"Ožbot","name":"Ožbot, Miha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Igor","familyName":"Škrjanc","name":"Škrjanc, Igor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vitomir","familyName":"Štruc","name":"Štruc, Vitomir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Neuro-Fuzzy System for Interpretable Long-Term Stock Market Forecasting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:33:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:58:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In the complex landscape of multivariate time series forecasting, achieving both accuracy and interpretability remains a significant challenge. This paper introduces the Fuzzy Transformer (Fuzzformer), a novel recurrent neural network architecture combined with multi-head self-attention and fuzzy inference systems to analyze multivariate stock market data and conduct long-term time series forecasting. The method leverages LSTM networks and temporal attention to condense multivariate data into interpretable features suitable for fuzzy inference systems. The resulting architecture offers comparable forecasting performance to conventional models such as ARIMA and LSTM while providing meaningful information flow within the network. The method was examined on the real world stock market index S\\\u0026amp;P500. Initial results show potential for interpretable forecasting and identify current performance tradeoffs, suggesting practical application in understanding and forecasting stock market behavior."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Published in: ERK 2025 -- 34th International Electrotechnical and Computer Science Conference, Portoro\\v{z}, Slovenia, Sept. 25--26, 2025. Proceedings published by Dru\\v{s}tvo Slovenska sekcija IEEE. ISSN: 2591-0442 (online). 4 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00960","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:13:06Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:13:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:13:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00932","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00932","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00932","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Zaeed","name":"Zaeed, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanzima Z.","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Tanzima Z.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vladimir","familyName":"Inđić","name":"Inđić, Vladimir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Opal: A Modular Framework for Optimizing Performance using Analytics and LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T14:14:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:57:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) show promise for automated code optimization but struggle without performance context. This work introduces Opal, a modular framework that connects performance analytics insights with the vast body of published by guiding LLMs to generate informed, trustworthy optimizations. Unlike traditional performance tools that identify bottlenecks but stop short of actionable suggestions, Opal bridges this long-standing gap by linking dynamic insights from hardware counters and Roofline analysis to stall events to optimization decisions. We evaluate Opal across 1640 experiments on real-world GPU kernels and find that in over 98.5% of cases, even a single insight source yields speedups, ranging on average from 19.34% to 52.3%. Our prompt template produced correct code in all but one case, where a vague diagnostic caused an unsafe suggestion. By automatically optimizing GPU kernels using performance analytics and LLMs, Opal marks a leap toward democratizing expert-level performance engineering for all."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages and 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00932","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:12:24Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:12:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:12:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00881","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00881","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00881","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrizio","familyName":"Migliarini","name":"Migliarini, Patrizio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mashal Afzal","familyName":"Memon","name":"Memon, Mashal Afzal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco","familyName":"Autili","name":"Autili, Marco","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paola","familyName":"Inverardi","name":"Inverardi, Paola","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Advancing Automated Ethical Profiling in SE: a Zero-Shot Evaluation of LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:28:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:54:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into software engineering (SE) tools for tasks that extend beyond code synthesis, including judgment under uncertainty and reasoning in ethically significant contexts. We present a fully automated framework for assessing ethical reasoning capabilities across 16 LLMs in a zero-shot setting, using 30 real-world ethically charged scenarios. Each model is prompted to identify the most applicable ethical theory to an action, assess its moral acceptability, and explain the reasoning behind their choice. Responses are compared against expert ethicists' choices using inter-model agreement metrics. Our results show that LLMs achieve an average Theory Consistency Rate (TCR) of 73.3% and Binary Agreement Rate (BAR) on moral acceptability of 86.7%, with interpretable divergences concentrated in ethically ambiguous cases. A qualitative analysis of free-text explanations reveals strong conceptual convergence across models despite surface-level lexical diversity. These findings support the potential viability of LLMs as ethical inference engines within SE pipelines, enabling scalable, auditable, and adaptive integration of user-aligned ethical reasoning. Our focus is the Ethical Interpreter component of a broader profiling pipeline: we evaluate whether current LLMs exhibit sufficient interpretive stability and theory-consistent reasoning to support automated profiling."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at ASE 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00881","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:11:11Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:11:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:11:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyun-kyu","familyName":"Ko","name":"Ko, Hyun-kyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youbin","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Youbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihyeon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Jihyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongheok","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Dongheok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyeongjin","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Gyeongjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wonjun","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Wonjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyung","familyName":"Yi","name":"Yi, Hyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eunbyung","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Eunbyung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gather-Scatter Mamba: Accelerating Propagation with Efficient State Space Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T13:11:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:53:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"State Space Models (SSMs)-most notably RNNs-have historically played a central role in sequential modeling. Although attention mechanisms such as Transformers have since dominated due to their ability to model global context, their quadratic complexity and limited scalability make them less suited for long sequences. Video super-resolution (VSR) methods have traditionally relied on recurrent architectures to propagate features across frames. However, such approaches suffer from well-known issues including vanishing gradients, lack of parallelism, and slow inference speed. Recent advances in selective SSMs like Mamba offer a compelling alternative: by enabling input-dependent state transitions with linear-time complexity, Mamba mitigates these issues while maintaining strong long-range modeling capabilities. Despite this potential, Mamba alone struggles to capture fine-grained spatial dependencies due to its causal nature and lack of explicit context aggregation. To address this, we propose a hybrid architecture that combines shifted window self-attention for spatial context aggregation with Mamba-based selective scanning for efficient temporal propagation. Furthermore, we introduce Gather-Scatter Mamba (GSM), an alignment-aware mechanism that warps features toward a center anchor frame within the temporal window before Mamba propagation and scatters them back afterward, effectively reducing occlusion artifacts and ensuring effective redistribution of aggregated information across all frames. The official implementation is provided at: https://github.com/Ko-Lani/GSMamba."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Code: \\url{https://github.com/Ko-Lani/GSMamba}"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:10:43Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:10:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:10:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00820","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00820","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00820","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangtao","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Xiangtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Rongyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuaizheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shuaizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingchen","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lingchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"NSARM: Next-Scale Autoregressive Modeling for Robust Real-World Image Super-Resolution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T12:29:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:50:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Most recent real-world image super-resolution (Real-ISR) methods employ pre-trained text-to-image (T2I) diffusion models to synthesize the high-quality image either from random Gaussian noise, which yields realistic results but is slow due to iterative denoising, or directly from the input low-quality image, which is efficient but at the price of lower output quality. These approaches train ControlNet or LoRA modules while keeping the pre-trained model fixed, which often introduces over-enhanced artifacts and hallucinations, suffering from the robustness to inputs of varying degradations. Recent visual autoregressive (AR) models, such as pre-trained Infinity, can provide strong T2I generation capabilities while offering superior efficiency by using the bitwise next-scale prediction strategy. Building upon next-scale prediction, we introduce a robust Real-ISR framework, namely Next-Scale Autoregressive Modeling (NSARM). Specifically, we train NSARM in two stages: a transformation network is first trained to map the input low-quality image to preliminary scales, followed by an end-to-end full-model fine-tuning. Such a comprehensive fine-tuning enhances the robustness of NSARM in Real-ISR tasks without compromising its generative capability. Extensive quantitative and qualitative evaluations demonstrate that as a pure AR model, NSARM achieves superior visual results over existing Real-ISR methods while maintaining a fast inference speed. Most importantly, it demonstrates much higher robustness to the quality of input images, showing stronger generalization performance. Project page: https://github.com/Xiangtaokong/NSARM"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00820","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:09:42Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:09:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:09:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00710","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00710","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00710","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihong","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Yihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Lecture notes: Biological propagation via reaction-diffusion equations with nonlocal diffusion and free boundary"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Analysis of PDEs (math.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35K57, 35R20, 35R35","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T09:40:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:43:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"These notes are based on the lectures given in a mini-course at VIASM (Vietnam Institute for Advanced Study in Mathematics) 2025 Summer School. They give a brief account of the theory (with detailed proofs) for propagation governed by a nonlocal reaction-diffusion model with free boundaries in one space dimension. The main part is concerned with a KPP reaction term, though the basic results on the existence and uniqueness of solutions as well as on the comparison principles are for more general situations. The contents are mostly taken from published recent works of the author with several collaborators, where the kernel function was assumed to be symmetric: J(x)=J(-x). When J(x) is not symmetric, significant differences may arise in the dynamics of the model, as shown in several preprints quoted in the references at the end of these notes, but many of the existing techniques can be easily extended to cover the \"weakly non-symmetric case\", and this is done here with all the necessary details."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00710","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:06:57Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:06:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:06:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00658","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00658","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00658","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beomsu","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Beomsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byunghee","familyName":"Cha","name":"Cha, Byunghee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jong Chul","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jong Chul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Align Your Tangent: Training Better Consistency Models via Manifold-Aligned Tangents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:35:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:39:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With diffusion and flow matching models achieving state-of-the-art generating performance, the interest of the community now turned to reducing the inference time without sacrificing sample quality. Consistency Models (CMs), which are trained to be consistent on diffusion or probability flow ordinary differential equation (PF-ODE) trajectories, enable one or two-step flow or diffusion sampling. However, CMs typically require prolonged training with large batch sizes to obtain competitive sample quality. In this paper, we examine the training dynamics of CMs near convergence and discover that CM tangents -- CM output update directions -- are quite oscillatory, in the sense that they move parallel to the data manifold, not towards the manifold. To mitigate oscillatory tangents, we propose a new loss function, called the manifold feature distance (MFD), which provides manifold-aligned tangents that point toward the data manifold. Consequently, our method -- dubbed Align Your Tangent (AYT) -- can accelerate CM training by orders of magnitude and even out-perform the learned perceptual image patch similarity metric (LPIPS). Furthermore, we find that our loss enables training with extremely small batch sizes without compromising sample quality. Code: https://github.com/1202kbs/AYT"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00658","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:05:19Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:05:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:05:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00636","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00636","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00636","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Devoto","name":"Devoto, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maximilian","familyName":"Jeblick","name":"Jeblick, Maximilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Jégou","name":"Jégou, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Expected Attention: KV Cache Compression by Estimating Attention from Future Queries Distribution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T08:12:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:37:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Memory consumption of the Key-Value (KV) cache represents a major bottleneck for efficient large language model inference. While attention-score-based KV cache pruning shows promise, it faces critical practical limitations: attention scores from future tokens are unavailable during compression, and modern implementations like Flash Attention do not materialize the full attention matrix, making past scores inaccessible. To overcome these challenges, we introduce $\\textbf{Expected Attention, a training-free compression method}$ that estimates KV pairs importance by predicting how future queries will attend to them. Our approach leverages the distributional properties of LLM activations to compute expected attention scores in closed form for each KV pair. These scores enable principled ranking and pruning of KV pairs with minimal impact on the residual stream, achieving effective compression without performance degradation. Importantly, our method operates seamlessly across both prefilling and decoding phases, consistently outperforming state-of-the-art baselines in both scenarios. Finally, $\\textbf{we release KVPress, a comprehensive library to enable researchers to implement and benchmark KV cache compression methods, already including more than 20 techniques}$."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00636","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:04:43Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:04:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:04:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00537","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00537","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00537","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nandan Kumar","familyName":"Jha","name":"Jha, Nandan Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon","familyName":"Reagen","name":"Reagen, Brandon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectral Scaling Laws in Language Models: How Effectively Do Feed-Forward Networks Use Their Latent Space?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T05:38:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:30:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As large language models (LLMs) scale, the question is not only how large they become, but how much of their capacity is effectively utilized. Existing scaling laws relate model size to loss, yet overlook how components exploit their latent space. We study feed-forward networks (FFNs) and recast width selection as a spectral utilization problem. Using a lightweight diagnostic suite -- Hard Rank (participation ratio), Soft Rank (Shannon rank), Spectral Concentration, and the composite Spectral Utilization Index (SUI) -- we quantify how many latent directions are meaningfully activated across LLaMA, GPT-2, and nGPT families. Our key finding is an asymmetric spectral scaling law: soft rank follows an almost perfect power law with FFN width, while hard rank grows only sublinearly and with high variance. This asymmetry suggests that widening FFNs mostly adds low-energy tail directions, while dominant-mode subspaces saturate early. Moreover, at larger widths, variance further collapses into a narrow subspace, leaving much of the latent space under-utilized. These results recast FFN width selection as a principled trade-off between tail capacity and dominant-mode capacity, offering concrete guidance for inference-efficient LLM design."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Main Conference (Long paper)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00537","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:02:17Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:02:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:02:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00482","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00482","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00482","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yawen","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Yawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Masaya","familyName":"Tsunokake","name":"Tsunokake, Masaya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuta","familyName":"Koreeda","name":"Koreeda, Yuta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ekant Muljibhai","familyName":"Amin","name":"Amin, Ekant Muljibhai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Sumiyoshi","name":"Sumiyoshi, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasuhiro","familyName":"Sogawa","name":"Sogawa, Yasuhiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agent Fine-tuning through Distillation for Domain-specific LLMs in Microdomains"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T04:04:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:26:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agentic large language models (LLMs) have become prominent for autonomously interacting with external environments and performing multi-step reasoning tasks. Most approaches leverage these capabilities via in-context learning with few-shot prompts, but this often results in lengthy inputs and higher computational costs. Agent fine-tuning offers an alternative by enabling LLMs to internalize procedural reasoning and domain-specific knowledge through training on relevant data and demonstration trajectories. While prior studies have focused on general domains, their effectiveness in specialized technical microdomains remains unclear. This paper explores agent fine-tuning for domain adaptation within Hitachi's JP1 middleware, a microdomain for specialized IT operations. We fine-tuned LLMs using JP1-specific datasets derived from domain manuals and distilled reasoning trajectories generated by LLMs themselves, enhancing decision making accuracy and search efficiency. During inference, we used an agentic prompt with retrieval-augmented generation and introduced a context-answer extractor to improve information relevance. On JP1 certification exam questions, our method achieved a 14% performance improvement over the base model, demonstrating the potential of agent fine-tuning for domain-specific reasoning in complex microdomains."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by AIxB 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00482","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T02:00:48Z","registered":"2025-10-02T02:00:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T02:00:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00392","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00392","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00392","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Omer","familyName":"Latif","name":"Latif, Muhammad Omer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayat","familyName":"Ullah","name":"Ullah, Hayat","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Ali","familyName":"Shafique","name":"Shafique, Muhammad Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihua","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Zhihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Deep Learning Pipeline for Epilepsy Genomic Analysis Using GPT-2 XL and NVIDIA H100"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Genomics (q-bio.GN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-01T01:07:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:17:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Epilepsy is a chronic neurological condition characterized by recurrent seizures, with global prevalence estimated at 50 million people worldwide. While progress in high-throughput sequencing has allowed for broad-based transcriptomic profiling of brain tissues, the deciphering of these highly complex datasets remains one of the challenges. To address this issue, in this paper we propose a new analysis pipeline that integrates the power of deep learning strategies with GPU-acceleration computation for investigating Gene expression patterns in epilepsy. Specifically, our proposed approach employs GPT-2 XL, a transformer-based Large Language Model (LLM) with 1.5 billion parameters for genomic sequence analysis over the latest NVIDIA H100 Tensor Core GPUs based on Hopper architecture. Our proposed method enables efficient preprocessing of RNA sequence data, gene sequence encoding, and subsequent pattern identification. We conducted experiments on two epilepsy datasets including GEO accession GSE264537 and GSE275235. The obtained results reveal several significant transcriptomic modifications, including reduced hippocampal astrogliosis after ketogenic diet treatment as well as restored excitatory-inhibitory signaling equilibrium in zebrafish epilepsy model. Moreover, our results highlight the effectiveness of leveraging LLMs in combination with advanced hardware acceleration for transcriptomic characterization in neurological diseases."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00392","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:58:35Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:58:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:58:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00326","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00326","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00326","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassen","familyName":"Dhrif","name":"Dhrif, Hassen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reasoning-Aware Prompt Orchestration: A Foundation Model for Multi-Agent Language Model Coordination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:33:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:13:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of large language models has enabled sophisticated multi-agent systems, yet coordinating their reasoning capabilities through prompt engineering remains challenging. We present a theoretically-grounded framework for dynamic prompt orchestration that enhances reasoning across multiple specialized agents. This framework addresses three core challenges: logical consistency preservation during agent transitions, reasoning-aware prompt adaptation, and scalable coordination of distributed inference.\n Our approach formalizes agent states using prompt templates, reasoning context vectors, and capability matrices. We prove system convergence to stable coordination patterns when step sizes satisfy $α\u0026lt; \\frac{1}{2L}$ where $L$ is the Lipschitz constant of the state transition function. We implement this through a distributed architecture that dynamically routes reasoning tasks while maintaining semantic coherence.\n Experimental results on 1,000 synthetic multi-agent conversations demonstrate a 42% reduction in reasoning latency, a 23% improvement in logical consistency measured by ROUGE-L score, and an 89% success rate for task completion without context loss across agent transitions. Ablation studies identify the consensus mechanism as the primary performance driver, while revealing limitations: performance degrades beyond 10 agent transitions, and the system requires 76.5GB memory for 1,000 concurrent agents. These findings establish a new paradigm for scalable reasoning in multi-agent systems, providing theoretical foundations for understanding reasoning emergence across coordinated language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00326","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:57:00Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:57:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:57:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00313","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00313","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00313","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tanmay","familyName":"Khandelwal","name":"Khandelwal, Tanmay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Magdalena","familyName":"Fuentes","name":"Fuentes, Magdalena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Post-Training Quantization for Audio Diffusion Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T22:09:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:13:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Transformers (DiTs) enable high-quality audio synthesis but are often computationally intensive and require substantial storage, which limits their practical deployment. In this paper, we present a comprehensive evaluation of post-training quantization (PTQ) techniques for audio DiTs, analyzing the trade-offs between static and dynamic quantization schemes. We explore two practical extensions (1) a denoising-timestep-aware smoothing method that adapts quantization scales per-input-channel and timestep to mitigate activation outliers, and (2) a lightweight low-rank adapter (LoRA)-based branch derived from singular value decomposition (SVD) to compensate for residual weight errors. Using Stable Audio Open we benchmark W8A8 and W4A8 configurations across objective metrics and human perceptual ratings. Our results show that dynamic quantization preserves fidelity even at lower precision, while static methods remain competitive with lower latency. Overall, our findings show that low-precision DiTs can retain high-fidelity generation while reducing memory usage by up to 79%."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 4 figures, accepted at IEEE Workshop on Applications of Signal Processing to Audio and Acoustics (WASPAA) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00313","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:41Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:56:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00297","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00297","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00297","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikram","familyName":"Krishnamurthy","name":"Krishnamurthy, Vikram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luke","familyName":"Snow","name":"Snow, Luke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Malliavin Calculus with Weak Derivatives for Counterfactual Stochastic Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T21:37:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:12:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study counterfactual stochastic optimization of conditional loss functionals under misspecified and noisy gradient information. The difficulty is that when the conditioning event has vanishing or zero probability, naive Monte Carlo estimators are prohibitively inefficient; kernel smoothing, though common, suffers from slow convergence. We propose a two-stage kernel-free methodology. First, we show using Malliavin calculus that the conditional loss functional of a diffusion process admits an exact representation as a Skorohod integral, yielding variance comparable to classical Monte-Carlo variance. Second, we establish that a weak derivative estimate of the conditional loss functional with respect to model parameters can be evaluated with constant variance, in contrast to the widely used score function method whose variance grows linearly in the sample path length. Together, these results yield an efficient framework for counterfactual conditional stochastic gradient algorithms in rare-event regimes."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00297","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:56:18Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:56:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:56:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00270","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00270","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00270","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tyler","familyName":"Hanks","name":"Hanks, Tyler","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hans","familyName":"Riess","name":"Riess, Hans","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Cohen","name":"Cohen, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matthew","familyName":"Hale","name":"Hale, Matthew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Fairbanks","name":"Fairbanks, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Asynchronous Nonlinear Sheaf Diffusion for Multi-Agent Coordination"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"93A16 (Primary), 55N30, 05C50 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:44:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:10:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Cellular sheaves and sheaf Laplacians provide a far-reaching generalization of graphs and graph Laplacians, resulting in a wide array of applications ranging from machine learning to multi-agent control. In the context of multi-agent systems, so called coordination sheaves provide a unifying formalism that models heterogeneous agents and coordination goals over undirected communication topologies, and applying sheaf diffusion drives agents to achieve their coordination goals. Existing literature on sheaf diffusion assumes that agents can communicate and compute updates synchronously, which is an unrealistic assumption in many scenarios where communication delays or heterogeneous agents with different compute capabilities cause disagreement among agents. To address these challenges, we introduce asynchronous nonlinear sheaf diffusion. Specifically, we show that under mild assumptions on the coordination sheaf and bounded delays in communication and computation, nonlinear sheaf diffusion converges to a minimizer of the Dirichlet energy of the coordination sheaf at a linear rate proportional to the delay bound. We further show that this linear convergence is attained from arbitrary initial conditions and the analysis depends on the spectrum of the sheaf Laplacian in a manner that generalizes the standard graph Laplacian case. We provide several numerical simulations to validate our theoretical results."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00270","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:55:39Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:55:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:55:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00268","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00268","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00268","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhexiong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhexiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Diane","familyName":"Litman","name":"Litman, Diane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Efficient Layer-wise LLM Fine-tuning for Revision Intention Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T20:42:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:09:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown extraordinary success across various text generation tasks; however, their potential for simple yet essential text classification remains underexplored, as LLM pre-training tends to emphasize generation over classification. While LLMs with instruction tuning can transform classification into a generation task, they often struggle to categorize nuanced texts. One such example is text revision, which involves nuanced edits between pairs of texts. Although simply fine-tuning LLMs for revision classification seems plausible, it requires a large amount of revision annotations, which are exceptionally expensive and scarce in the community. To address this issue, we introduce a plug-and-play layer-wise parameter-efficient fine-tuning (PEFT) framework, i.e., IR-Tuning, which fine-tunes a subset of important LLM layers that are dynamically selected based on their gradient norm distribution, while freezing those of redundant layers. Extensive experiments suggest that IR-Tuning surpasses several layer-wise PEFT baselines over diverse text revisions, while achieving fast convergence, low GPU memory consumption, and effectiveness on small revision corpora."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"In The Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing (EMNLP), November 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00268","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:55:36Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:55:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:55:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhanda","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qidong","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Qidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaoyao","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yaoyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shang","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gennady","familyName":"Pekhimenko","name":"Pekhimenko, Gennady","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LoRAFusion: Efficient LoRA Fine-Tuning for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:26:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:07:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767295.3769331","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA) has become the leading Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (PEFT) method for Large Language Models (LLMs), as it significantly reduces GPU memory usage while maintaining competitive fine-tuned model quality on downstream tasks. Despite these benefits, we identify two key inefficiencies in existing LoRA fine-tuning systems. First, they incur substantial runtime overhead due to redundant memory accesses on large activation tensors. Second, they miss the opportunity to concurrently fine-tune multiple independent LoRA adapters that share the same base model on the same set of GPUs. This leads to missed performance gains such as reduced pipeline bubbles, better communication overlap, and improved GPU load balance.\n To address these issues, we introduce LoRAFusion, an efficient LoRA fine-tuning system for LLMs. At the kernel level, we propose a graph-splitting method that fuses memory-bound operations. This design eliminates unnecessary memory accesses and preserves the performance of compute-bound GEMMs without incurring the cost of recomputation or synchronization. At the scheduling level, LoRAFusion introduces an adaptive batching algorithm for multi-job fine-tuning. It first splits LoRA adapters into groups to intentionally stagger batch execution across jobs, and then solves a bin-packing problem within each group to generate balanced, dependency-aware microbatches. LoRAFusion achieves up to $1.96\\times$ ($1.47\\times$ on average) end-to-end speedup compared to Megatron-LM, and up to $1.46\\times$ ($1.29\\times$ on average) improvement over mLoRA, the state-of-the-art multi-LoRA fine-tuning system. Our fused kernel achieves up to $1.39\\times$ ($1.27\\times$ on average) kernel performance improvement and can directly serve as a plug-and-play replacement in existing LoRA systems. We open-source LoRAFusion at https://github.com/CentML/lorafusion."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by EuroSys 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-02T01:54:03Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:54:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:54:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00184","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00184","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00184","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyan","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Xiaoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Itamar","familyName":"Pres","name":"Pres, Itamar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntian","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yuntian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenhao","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Chenhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stuart","familyName":"Shieber","name":"Shieber, Stuart","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fernanda","familyName":"Viégas","name":"Viégas, Fernanda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Martin","familyName":"Wattenberg","name":"Wattenberg, Martin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Why Can't Transformers Learn Multiplication? Reverse-Engineering Reveals Long-Range Dependency Pitfalls"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T19:03:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:06:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Language models are increasingly capable, yet still fail at a seemingly simple task of multi-digit multiplication. In this work, we study why, by reverse-engineering a model that successfully learns multiplication via \\emph{implicit chain-of-thought}, and report three findings: (1) Evidence of long-range structure: Logit attributions and linear probes indicate that the model encodes the necessary long-range dependencies for multi-digit multiplication. (2) Mechanism: the model encodes long-range dependencies using attention to construct a directed acyclic graph to ``cache'' and ``retrieve'' pairwise partial products. (3) Geometry: the model implements partial products in attention heads by forming Minkowski sums between pairs of digits, and digits are represented using a Fourier basis, both of which are intuitive and efficient representations that the standard fine-tuning model lacks. With these insights, we revisit the learning dynamics of standard fine-tuning and find that the model converges to a local optimum that lacks the required long-range dependencies. We further validate this understanding by introducing an auxiliary loss that predicts the ``running sum'' via a linear regression probe, which provides an inductive bias that enables the model to successfully learn multi-digit multiplication. In summary, by reverse-engineering the mechanisms of an implicit chain-of-thought model we uncover a pitfall for learning long-range dependencies in Transformers and provide an example of how the correct inductive bias can address this issue."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00184","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:53:31Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:53:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:53:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00129","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00129","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00129","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengkui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hengkui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liujiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liujiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihua","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jihua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keke","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Keke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Shuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Renle","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Renle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Dahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingyu","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Lingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bruce","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Bruce","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Zhan","name":"Zhan, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqiang","familyName":"Niu","name":"Niu, Jiaqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinglong","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Xinglong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yaqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenjun","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Wenjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Panpan","familyName":"Chi","name":"Chi, Panpan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ken","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ken","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Hengyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingsi","familyName":"Xin","name":"Xin, Yingsi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongfeng","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yongfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuexin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manqi","familyName":"Ruan","name":"Ruan, Manqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ningtao","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Ningtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaohua","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Xiaohua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weipeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Weipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BigBang-Proton Technical Report: Next-Word-Prediction is Scientific Multitask Learner"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6; I.2.7; J.2; I.6.3; K.4.1","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T05, 68T50, 00A69, 94A99","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-30T18:09:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:03:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce BigBang-Proton, a unified sequence-based architecture for auto-regressive language modeling pretrained on cross-scale, cross-structure, cross-discipline real-world scientific tasks to construct a scientific multi-task learner. BigBang-Proton incorporates three fundamental innovations compared to mainstream general-purpose LLMs: Theory-Experiment Learning paradigm aligns large-scale numerical experimental data with theoretical text corpora; Binary Patch Encoding replaces byte pair encoding(BPE) tokenization; Monte Carlo Attention substitutes traditional transformer architectures. Through next-word-prediction pretraining on cross-discipline scientific datasets of real-world problems mixed with general textual corpus, followed by fine-tuning and inference on downstream tasks, BigBang-Proton demonstrates 100\\% accuracy in up to 50-digit arithmetic addition operations, performance on par with leading specialized models in particle physics jet tagging, matching MAE of specialized models in inter-atomic potential simulation, performance comparable to traditional spatiotemporal models in water quality prediction, and benchmark-exceeding performance in genome modeling. These results prove that language-guided scientific computing can match or exceed the performance of task-specific scientific models while maintaining multitask learning capabilities. We further hypothesize to scale the pretraining to the universe scale as a fundamental step toward developing material world foundational model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"93 pages, 39 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00129","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:52:09Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:52:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:52:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.00028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.00028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ye","familyName":"Qiao","name":"Qiao, Ye","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haocheng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Haocheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sitao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Sitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rethinking RoPE Scaling in Quantized LLM: Theory, Outlier, and Channel-Band Analysis with Weight Rescaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T01:23:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-02T00:00:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Extending the context window support of large language models (LLMs) is crucial for tasks with long-distance dependencies. RoPE-based interpolation and extrapolation methods, such as linear scaling and frequency-aware schemes, enable longer input length support without retraining, while post-training quantization (PTQ) makes deployment practical. However, we show that combining RoPE position interpolation (PI) with PTQ degrades accuracy due to coupled effects including long-context aliasing, dynamic-range dilation, anisotropy from axis-aligned quantizers vs. rotated RoPE pairs, and outlier shifting that produces position-dependent logit noise. We provide, to the best of our knowledge, the first systematic analysis of the PI+PTQ approach and introduce two practical diagnostics: interpolation pressure (per-band sensitivity to phase scaling) and tail-inflation ratios (outlier shift from short to long contexts). Following the analysis results, we propose Q-ROAR (Quantization, RoPE-interpolation, and Outlier Aware Rescaling), a weight-only, interpolation-aware stabilization of PI for quantized LLMs. Q-ROAR groups RoPE dimensions into a small number of frequency bands and performs a lightweight search over per-band scales for Key and Query weights (with an optional symmetric variant to preserve logit scale). The search is guided by our diagnostics and uses a tiny long-context development dataset, requiring no fine-tuning to the model, no architecture or kernel changes, and no additional deployment overhead. Empirically, Q-ROAR reduces the model's perplexity on long-context workloads by more than 14%, while preserving short-context performance, inference throughput, and compatibility with existing LLM system stacks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.00028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-02T01:49:40Z","registered":"2025-10-02T01:49:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-02T01:49:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":55,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-01T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-02T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28%28GPU+OR+kernel+OR+inference+OR+serving+OR+quantization+OR+parallel+OR+cache+OR+communication%29+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+diffusion%29%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered"}}